        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "3f32749c-d279-5b29-ace4-baaf0ef7a4d3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Hovsep Pushman, Armen Chakmakian, John Singer Sargent, George de Forest Brush\n- Organizations: Grand Central Art Galleries, Shadowfax, TruArt Records, GRAMMY Awards\n- Products: Esperanto, Ceremonies, Caravans, Buddha Bar, Buddha Bar IV\n- Roles: artist, musician, composer, recording artist, producer, keyboardist\n\nFacts:\n- Hovsep Pushman was born on May 9, 1877, and he was an American artist of Armenian background.\n- In 1928, John Singer Sargent's posthumous show took place at the Grand Central Art Galleries.\n- Hovsep Pushman, George de Forest Brush, and John Singer Sargent were closely associated with the Grand Central Art Galleries.\n- Hovsep Pushman was known for contemplative still lifes and sensitive portraits of women, often in exotic dress.\n- Hovsep Pushman died on February 13, 1966.\n- Armen John Chakmakian was born on February 11, 1966, in Glendale, California, and he is an Armenian-American musician, composer, recording artist, and producer.\n- Armen Chakmakian was formerly the keyboardist for Shadowfax.\n- Shadowfax's 1992 CD Esperanto was nominated for a Best New Age Album GRAMMY Award.\n- Armen Chakmakian released the solo album Ceremonies on TruArt Records in 1998.\n- Two tracks from Ceremonies, Gypsy Rain and Distant Lands, also appeared on the compilation albums Buddha Bar and Buddha Bar IV, respectively.\n- Armen Chakmakian released the solo album Caravans on TruArt Records in 2004.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a40156e9-962e-50ed-9ba3-b80c4572c428",
        "text": "Page: Hovsep Pushman. Sentence: Hovsep Pushman (Armenian: Յովսէփ Փուշման ; May 9, 1877 – February 13, 1966) was an American artist of Armenian background.\nPage: Hovsep Pushman. Sentence: He was known for his contemplative still lifes and sensitive portraits of women, often in exotic dress.\nPage: Armen Chakmakian. Sentence: Armen John Chakmakian (Armenian: Արմեն Չաքմաքյան ; born (February 11, 1966) in Glendale, California) is an Armenian-American musician, composer, recording artist, and producer.\nPage: Armen Chakmakian. Sentence: Formerly the keyboardist for the GRAMMY® award-winning band Shadowfax, their 1992 CD, \"Esperanto\" was nominated for a Best New Age Album GRAMMY® Award.\nPage: Armen Chakmakian. Sentence: He has released two solo albums on his label TruArt Records: \"Ceremonies\" (1998); \"Caravans\" (2004).\nPage: Armen Chakmakian. Sentence: Two tracks from \"Ceremonies\", \"Gypsy Rain\" and \"Distant Lands\", also appear on the famed \"Buddha Bar\" and \"Buddha Bar IV\" compilation albums, respectively.\nPage: Grand Central Art Galleries. Sentence: Artists closely associated with the Grand Central Art Galleries included Hovsep Pushman, George de Forest Brush, and especially Sargent, whose posthumous show took place there in 1928.",
